{"name":"Atomic","hex":"#3d4b52","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"atomic","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to atoms; composed of atoms; monatomic.","Employing or relating to nuclear energy or processes."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An atomic operation."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/atomic"}],"entries":[{"title":"Atom","blocks":[{"text":"Atoms are the basic particles of the chemical elements and the fundamental building blocks of matter. An atom consists of a nucleus of protons and generally neutrons, surrounded by an electromagnetically bound swarm of electrons. The chemical elements are distinguished from each other by the number of protons that are in their atoms. For example, any atom that contains 11 protons is sodium, and any atom that contains 29 protons is copper. Atoms with the same number of protons but a different number of neutrons are called isotopes of the same element."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Helium_atom_QM.svg/330px-Helium_atom_QM.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atom","wikidata":"Q9121"},{"title":"Atomic physics","blocks":[{"text":"Atomic physics is the field of physics that studies atoms as an isolated system of electrons and an atomic nucleus. Atomic physics typically refers to the study of atomic structure and the interaction between atoms. It is primarily concerned with the way in which electrons are arranged around the nucleus and\nthe processes by which these arrangements change. This comprises ions, neutral atoms and, unless otherwise stated, it can be assumed that the term atom includes ions."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atomic_physics","wikidata":"Q26383"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Daltons_symbols.gif/500px-Daltons_symbols.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Daltons_symbols.gif/500px-Daltons_symbols.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Various atoms and molecules from A New System of Chemical Philosophy (John Dalton, 1808)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADaltons_symbols.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Bohr_atom_animation_2.gif/250px-Bohr_atom_animation_2.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/17/Bohr_atom_animation_2.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Bohr_atom_animation_2.gif/250px-Bohr_atom_animation_2.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Bohr model of the atom, with an electron making instantaneous \"quantum leaps\" from one orbit to another with gain or loss of energy. This model of electrons in orbits is obsolete.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABohr_atom_animation_2.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Atomic-orbital-clouds_spdf_m0.png/500px-Atomic-orbital-clouds_spdf_m0.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Atomic-orbital-clouds_spdf_m0.png/1280px-Atomic-orbital-clouds_spdf_m0.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Atomic-orbital-clouds_spdf_m0.png/500px-Atomic-orbital-clouds_spdf_m0.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"3D views of some hydrogen-like atomic orbitals showing probability density and phase (g orbitals and higher are not shown)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A